turning ideas into action  the conference on the future of europe  has seen panels of ordinary citizens  formulate and deliver nearly 200  proposals on how to improve the eu  now they're being fine-tuned in  strasbourg where working groups brought  panel representatives together with  members of the european and national  parliaments eu institutions and civil  society they gave us a lot of concrete  issues for example about transparency  about the question that the language of  europe is sometimes bureaucratic and not  understandable the conference plenary  debated draft proposals on youth and  education and how to balance the economy  and social justice in the age of digital  transformation citizens challenged the  input of politicians and stakeholders  insisting their recommendations should  not be watered down although we as  citizens do welcome the many additions  to our recommendations  we do fear that our recommendations are  in risk of drowning in political agendas  plenary also examined new  recommendations on migration health and  climate change and debated the eu's  place in the world democracy rights and  values  there are a lot of good proposals but  some of them remain quite vague we need  to ensure  that we don't just produce proposals  where it will be too easy to dismiss  them after the conference plenui will  meet again to finalize conclusions to be  handed over to the presidents of the  european parliament commission and  council there is naturally still a lot  of work to do what i have as conclusion  of the work of the two last  days  is that we have found  together a method based on the  recommendations of the citizens  to come to real conclusions for this  conference  [Applause]  you
 

